  add({id:'digestive-system',title:'Digestive System',category:'human-physiology',summary:'Connect movement, secretion and hydrolysis along the human digestive tract.',aliases:['alimentary canal','gut','digestion'],relatedTopics:['nutrient-absorption','enzymes','biomolecules'],prerequisites:['enzymes'],sources:['es-digestion','es-absorption']},[
    'Food must be moved, mixed and broken into usable molecules. The mouth, stomach and intestines cooperate with secretions from other organs. Digestion changes food; absorption moves substances across the gut lining into the body.',
    'A digestive tract is not one tank of acid. Successive compartments provide different chemical conditions, so enzymes that work well in one region need not work well in another. Movement coordinates exposure to these environments.',
    'Trace a food bolus through mouth, esophagus, stomach, small intestine and colon. Add secretion arrows from liver and pancreas without sending the food through those organs. This process map shows routes, not anatomical scale.',
    'Peristalsis propels contents while segmentation mixes them. Gastric acid supports protein digestion, and pancreatic bicarbonate helps neutralize acidic chyme entering the duodenum. Pancreatic and brush-border enzymes complete much digestion in the small intestine.',
    'Enteric circuits and signals such as gastrin, secretin and cholecystokinin coordinate secretion and gastric emptying. Protease precursors help limit inappropriate tissue digestion. Bile emulsifies lipids and supports micelles but is not a hydrolytic enzyme.',
    'Comparing the same food before and after crushing tests surface area, not a new chemical bond-breaking mechanism. A useful digestion investigation controls enzyme amount, pH, temperature and time before attributing a rate difference to mixing.'
  ],[['Peristalsis','Coordinated contractions that propel material through a tubular organ.'],['Chyme','Partly processed stomach contents entering the small intestine.'],['Hydrolysis','Chemical bond cleavage involving water.']],[['Prepare','Chewing and saliva increase access to food substrates.'],['Process','Stomach mixing and secretions begin substantial protein digestion.'],['Complete','Intestinal secretions finish breakdown under different chemical conditions.'],['Absorb','Small molecules cross the epithelium; residues continue into the colon.']],[
    ['What is the direct role of bile in lipid digestion?',['Disperse lipids and support micelle formation','Hydrolyze every triglyceride bond','Convert proteins into glucose'],0,'Bile supports lipid access and transport; lipases perform the hydrolysis.'],
    ['Why does pancreatic bicarbonate help intestinal digestion?',['It moderates acidic chyme for intestinal enzymes','It supplies all digestive enzyme active sites','It stops every intestinal contraction'],0,'Changing luminal pH helps create conditions suitable for pancreatic and intestinal enzymes.'],
    ['Which event is absorption rather than digestion?',['Glucose crosses the intestinal epithelium','A protease cleaves a peptide bond','Chewing fragments a food particle'],0,'Absorption means crossing an epithelial boundary, not merely breaking food down.']
  ],{curiosity:'Why is digestion divided into compartments?',objectives:['Separate digestion, absorption and elimination.','Connect changing gut conditions to enzyme action.','Interpret a controlled test of food surface area.'],mechanism:[
    ['Increase access','Chewing divides food and mixing repeatedly exposes its surfaces to secretions. This physical processing does not itself hydrolyze every food polymer. It can nevertheless accelerate chemical digestion by making substrate more accessible, provided enzymes and other required conditions are available.'],
    ['Change the reaction environment','The stomach and small intestine are chemically distinct compartments. Acid assists gastric protein processing, whereas bicarbonate buffers chyme arriving in the duodenum. Different enzymes therefore act in different environments rather than one universal enzyme digesting every substrate throughout the tract.'],
    ['Coordinate onward movement','Local enteric circuits and endocrine signals adjust mixing, secretion and emptying in response to contents. Breakdown products must then cross an epithelial barrier to become absorbed nutrients. Remaining material travels onward, so fast transit alone does not establish efficient digestion or absorption.']
  ],misconception:['All digestion occurs in the stomach.','The stomach contributes important mixing and protein processing, but much chemical digestion and most nutrient absorption occur in the small intestine. Accessory organs deliver secretions without becoming part of the route taken by food.'],investigation:{title:'Surface area changes access',context:'Synthetic enzyme-assay results after the same interval, with equal substrate mass, enzyme concentration, pH and temperature.',columns:['Preparation','Product formed (relative units)'],rows:[['Large pieces with enzyme','4'],['Small pieces with enzyme','10'],['Small pieces without enzyme','1']],question:'How much greater is product formation for small pieces, and what does the control establish?',hint:'Compare enzyme-containing samples, then ask what occurs without the catalyst.',answer:'Small pieces produce 2.5 times as much measured product. The no-enzyme control supports an enzyme-dependent contribution, but does not prove that surface area is the only changing property.',reasoning:['Divide ten by four to compare the matched enzyme-containing samples.','The no-enzyme sample forms much less measured product over time.','Check whether crushing also changed solubility before claiming surface area alone.'],limitation:'Invented relative readings illustrate experimental reasoning; they cannot predict digestion rates for a real meal.'},transfer:['Would more vigorous gut movement always improve nutrient uptake?','Consider residence time as well as mixing and transport capacity.','No. Mixing can improve access, but excessively rapid transit can shorten the time available for digestion and absorption. Transport capacity and secretions also constrain uptake.']});

  add({id:'nutrient-absorption',title:'Nutrient Absorption',category:'human-physiology',summary:'Follow nutrients through a polarized intestinal cell into blood or lymph.',aliases:['villi','microvilli','intestinal absorption','lacteal'],relatedTopics:['digestive-system','active-transport','membrane-transport'],prerequisites:['membrane-transport'],sources:['es-absorption']},[
    'Digested nutrients still have to cross the intestinal lining. Folds, villi and tiny microvilli enlarge the available surface. Different molecules use different transport routes, and many absorbed lipids reach blood through lymph first.',
    'An intestinal cell has two differently equipped sides. Entry from the gut and exit toward the circulation must both work. Enlarging the surface helps only when transport proteins, cellular energy and onward circulation can support the traffic.',
    'Follow glucose from the lumen across the apical membrane, through an epithelial cell and across its basolateral membrane. Compare a long-chain lipid route through a chylomicron and lacteal. Arrows distinguish membranes from blood vessels.',
    'Sodium-coupled uptake supports apical glucose entry; a basolateral sodium-potassium pump maintains the gradient. Fructose uses facilitated transport. Amino acids and small peptides have several transport routes. Monosaccharides enter portal blood, while chylomicrons enter intestinal lymphatics.',
    'Transport can saturate, so doubling luminal concentration need not double flux. Long-chain fatty acids and monoacylglycerols delivered from micelles are largely reassembled into triglycerides and packaged in chylomicrons. Many shorter-chain fatty acids follow a more direct portal route.',
    'An absorption model must distinguish loss of digestive enzymes from loss of epithelial transport capacity. Both can reduce nutrient delivery, but they predict different concentrations of intact food molecules and absorbable products remaining in the lumen.'
  ],[['Villus','A projection of intestinal mucosa containing blood vessels and a lymphatic lacteal.'],['Apical membrane','The epithelial surface facing the gut lumen.'],['Chylomicron','A lipoprotein particle carrying absorbed dietary lipids from intestinal cells.']],[['Digest','Generate molecules compatible with absorption pathways.'],['Enter','Use molecule-specific routes across the apical membrane.'],['Package or export','Move soluble products into portal blood or package long-chain lipids.'],['Distribute','Lymph carrying chylomicrons eventually returns to blood.']],[
    ['Why can glucose uptake depend indirectly on ATP?',['The sodium gradient is maintained by an ATP-driven pump','Glucose must be converted into ATP before crossing','Every glucose carrier directly hydrolyzes ATP'],0,'The basolateral pump maintains the sodium gradient that powers coupled apical uptake.'],
    ['Where do intestinal chylomicrons usually enter first?',['Lacteals and lymph','The stomach lumen','Red blood cells'],0,'Their initial transport is through intestinal lymphatics before joining the circulation.'],
    ['Why might more luminal glucose stop increasing uptake proportionally?',['Available transport pathways can become limiting','All glucose diffusion stops at high concentration','Villi disappear whenever glucose rises'],0,'Finite transporter capacity can produce saturation even while glucose remains available.']
  ],{curiosity:'Why are the two sides of a gut cell different?',objectives:['Trace glucose across both epithelial membranes.','Distinguish portal and lymphatic nutrient routes.','Recognize transport saturation in a data pattern.'],mechanism:[
    ['Present absorbable products','Digestion provides small molecules while folds and microvilli increase epithelial contact area. These adaptations are complementary rather than interchangeable: a large surface cannot absorb an unavailable digestion product, and adequate digestion does not guarantee intact membrane transport or blood supply.'],
    ['Couple entry to a gradient','Glucose can enter through a sodium-coupled apical transporter. A separate basolateral pump uses ATP to maintain a low intracellular sodium concentration. This arrangement makes uptake indirectly energy-dependent while keeping the coupled carrier distinct from the pump that powers the gradient.'],
    ['Complete the route','Nutrients must also leave the epithelial cell. Many soluble nutrients enter portal circulation, whereas reassembled long-chain lipids are packaged into chylomicrons and exported to lacteals. Accumulation in a cell alone is therefore not evidence of successful delivery to the rest of the body.']
  ],misconception:['All absorbed nutrients enter intestinal blood capillaries by the same route.','Transport pathways differ with molecular properties. Many soluble nutrients enter portal blood, while chylomicrons carrying long-chain dietary lipids initially enter lymph. This distinction concerns the early route, not a permanent separation from the bloodstream.'],investigation:{title:'A saturating absorption pathway',context:'Synthetic uptake measurements for equal epithelial areas over one minute; concentration and flux use arbitrary teaching units.',columns:['Luminal concentration (units)','Uptake (units/min)'],rows:[['1','3'],['2','5'],['4','6']],question:'Does doubling concentration from two to four double uptake, and what mechanism could explain the pattern?',hint:'Compare the flux ratio with the concentration ratio before proposing a cause.',answer:'Uptake rises only from five to six units per minute, a 20% increase. A finite transporter capacity could explain the flattening, but other limiting steps must also be tested.',reasoning:['Concentration doubles, whereas uptake increases by one of the original five units.','Finite transport capacity predicts a diminishing response as occupancy increases.','Maintained viability and basolateral export would help exclude other bottlenecks.'],limitation:'Invented values do not identify a specific transporter or separate saturation from inadequate energy supply.'},transfer:['What happens if the apical carrier works but basolateral export is blocked?','Follow the nutrient beyond its first membrane crossing.','Initial entry may occur, but delivery to blood is restricted and intracellular accumulation can eventually impede further uptake. Measuring epithelial content alone would overstate complete absorption.']});

  add({id:'endocrine-system',title:'Endocrine System',category:'human-physiology',summary:'Explain hormone specificity, signal amplification and feedback between organs.',aliases:['endocrine glands','hormone signaling','pituitary','thyroid'],relatedTopics:['homeostasis','insulin-glucagon','reproductive-anatomy'],prerequisites:['cell-membrane','protein-synthesis'],sources:['es-hormones']},[
    'Endocrine cells release hormones into the circulation. A hormone can travel widely yet affect only cells equipped to respond. These signals coordinate growth, metabolism, reproduction and water balance across organs that do not directly touch.',
    'Broadcasting a message is not the same as making every cell respond. Receptors and intracellular machinery decide whether a hormone is heard and what it means. Feedback then adjusts future secretion according to the resulting physiological state.',
    'Trace gland secretion, transport, receptor binding and target-cell response. Add a return arrow showing negative feedback. Keep a hormone molecule separate from the downstream relay it activates; one binding event may influence many cellular reactions.',
    'Many peptide hormones use membrane receptors and intracellular second messengers. Steroid hormones commonly bind intracellular receptors that regulate transcription. Thyroid hormones also act through intracellular receptors despite being amino-acid derivatives, illustrating why chemistry and receptor mechanism must be distinguished.',
    'Free hormone concentration, carrier binding, receptor abundance and clearance all influence response. Endocrine axes often include several interacting feedback loops, pulsatile secretion and time delays. A single blood concentration therefore cannot fully reveal secretion rate, receptor sensitivity or biological effect.',
    'A laboratory comparison of hormone amount and target response can distinguish altered secretion from altered sensitivity only with suitable controls. These principles explain experimental patterns; this lesson is not a guide to interpreting personal hormone tests.'
  ],[['Hormone','A signaling molecule released by cells to influence responsive target cells.'],['Receptor','A molecule that detects a signal and initiates or modifies a response.'],['Negative feedback','A response that opposes the initiating deviation or reduces upstream signaling.']],[['Release','An endocrine cell secretes a signal in response to input.'],['Recognize','Target cells bind the hormone through appropriate receptors.'],['Respond','Signaling changes enzyme activity, transport or gene expression.'],['Feed back','The resulting state influences later secretion.']],[
    ['Why can a circulating hormone affect only some cells?',['Only responsive cells have appropriate receptors and signaling machinery','Blood visits only those cells','Hormones act only where they were made'],0,'Target-cell receptor expression and downstream machinery determine responsiveness to a shared signal.'],
    ['Which feature fits a typical steroid-hormone mechanism?',['Regulation of transcription through an intracellular receptor','Direct translation into a new ribosome','Obligate passage through a sodium channel'],0,'Many steroid hormones regulate gene expression through intracellular hormone-receptor complexes.'],
    ['What does negative feedback usually accomplish in an endocrine axis?',['Limits an upstream drive when its effects increase','Makes every response grow without limit','Eliminates all fluctuation in secretion'],0,'Feedback reduces deviations or upstream stimulation without implying perfectly constant hormone concentrations.']
  ],{curiosity:'Why can a widespread signal have a selective effect?',objectives:['Connect receptor expression to target specificity.','Compare membrane and intracellular receptor pathways.','Use paired signal-response measurements to evaluate sensitivity.'],mechanism:[
    ['Secrete and distribute','Endocrine secretion puts a signal into circulating fluid, where dilution, binding proteins and clearance affect its availability. A hormone does not need to enter every cell it passes. Its distribution and its recognition by a target are different stages of communication.'],
    ['Convert recognition into action','A responsive cell has suitable receptors and downstream machinery. Membrane receptors can initiate relays that modify existing proteins, while intracellular receptors commonly regulate transcription. These are useful patterns, not a promise that every hormone in a chemical family follows one identical time course.'],
    ['Close a regulatory loop','Target responses can alter the stimulus or reduce signaling from upstream endocrine tissues. A delayed negative-feedback loop may fluctuate rather than remain perfectly flat. Interpreting an endocrine system therefore requires the relationship between input, output and time, not just one isolated hormone measurement.']
  ],misconception:['More hormone always causes a proportionally larger effect.','Responses depend on receptor number, signaling capacity, clearance and the current state of the target. Saturation or reduced sensitivity can blunt a response, so hormone concentration and physiological effect need not increase in direct proportion.'],investigation:{title:'Same signal, different response',context:'Fictional cultured-cell measurements using the same hormone exposure time; responses are arbitrary units, not clinical values.',columns:['Cell preparation','Hormone (units)','Response (units)'],rows:[['Receptors present','2','8'],['Receptors reduced','2','3'],['No hormone control','0','1']],question:'Which comparison isolates receptor availability, and why is a lower response not evidence of lower hormone supply?',hint:'Find the pair with the same supplied concentration but a different cell property.',answer:'The first two preparations receive two hormone units each, yet their responses differ. Reduced receptor availability is consistent with lower sensitivity without any reduction in the supplied signal.',reasoning:['The supplied hormone concentration is matched between the two receptor conditions.','The no-hormone condition estimates the response without the added signal.','Cell viability and downstream pathway function remain important control measurements.'],limitation:'These synthetic observations do not establish clinical endocrine sensitivity or exclude all changes caused by the receptor intervention.'},transfer:['Could changing hormone clearance alter concentration without changing secretion?','Treat blood concentration as the balance of entry and removal.','Yes. Slower removal can increase circulating concentration even at unchanged secretion. Binding, distribution and feedback may then modify the response, so concentration alone does not specify gland output.']});

  add({id:'insulin-glucagon',title:'Insulin & Glucagon',category:'human-physiology',summary:'Connect pancreatic sensing, liver glucose output and tissue uptake across fed and fasting states.',aliases:['blood glucose regulation','pancreatic islets','glycogen'],relatedTopics:['endocrine-system','homeostasis','cellular-respiration'],prerequisites:['homeostasis'],sources:['es-pancreas','es-insulin']},[
    'Blood glucose reflects both incoming fuel and its use or storage. Insulin and glucagon help coordinate these flows. Insulin generally favors storage and reduced liver glucose output; glucagon helps the liver make glucose available during fasting.',
    'Glucose concentration is a balance between entry and removal, not simply a reading of the last meal. A signal can lower that concentration by reducing liver output as well as by increasing uptake in selected tissues.',
    'Compare a fed-state route into glycogen and other stores with a fasting route out of the liver. Place insulin beside pancreatic beta cells and glucagon beside alpha cells. Arrows show regulated flows, not glucose created from nothing.',
    'Insulin promotes GLUT4 recruitment in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue and regulates fuel metabolism in the liver. Glucagon stimulates hepatic glycogen breakdown and gluconeogenesis. The hormones do not have identical direct actions in all tissues, and basal glucose uptake is not universally insulin-dependent.',
    'Beta-cell glucose metabolism links increased ATP availability to electrical activity, calcium entry and insulin secretion. Counter-regulation also involves other hormones and autonomic signals. Hepatic glycogen is finite, and gluconeogenesis rearranges supplied precursors rather than producing carbon without substrates.',
    'A simplified fed-versus-fasting model can explain opposing changes in glucose flows, but it cannot diagnose diabetes or prescribe food, medicines or insulin. Real regulation depends on timing, tissue sensitivity, activity and many interacting signals.'
  ],[['Beta cell','A pancreatic islet cell that secretes insulin.'],['Alpha cell','A pancreatic islet cell that secretes glucagon.'],['Gluconeogenesis','Production of glucose from suitable non-carbohydrate precursors.']],[['Sense','Islet cells respond to metabolic and regulatory inputs.'],['Store','Insulin favors uptake in selected tissues and storage pathways.'],['Release','Glucagon supports hepatic glucose availability during fasting.'],['Balance','Changing output and use feeds back onto glucose regulation.']],[
    ['How can insulin lower circulating glucose besides increasing muscle uptake?',['Reduce liver glucose output','Turn every cell into a glucose-producing cell','Convert glucose directly into a hormone'],0,'Insulin regulates hepatic metabolism as well as uptake in selected peripheral tissues.'],
    ['Which cells are the principal source of pancreatic glucagon?',['Alpha cells','Beta cells','Red blood cells'],0,'Pancreatic alpha cells secrete glucagon, while beta cells secrete insulin.'],
    ['Which statement about glucose uptake is accurate?',['Not every tissue requires insulin for basal glucose uptake','Every glucose transporter is GLUT4','Glucagon directly recruits GLUT4 in every tissue'],0,'Transporter expression differs among tissues, so insulin dependence is not universal.']
  ],{curiosity:'Can liver output matter as much as food input?',objectives:['Identify pancreatic sources of insulin and glucagon.','Separate hepatic glucose output from peripheral uptake.','Calculate a simplified glucose-pool balance.'],mechanism:[
    ['Detect a changing fuel state','Pancreatic islet cells integrate nutrient and neural inputs. In beta cells, glucose metabolism can connect the ATP-sensitive electrical state to calcium entry and secretion. This coupling supports a response to available fuel without making glucose the only influence on insulin release.'],
    ['Redistribute and store','Insulin increases GLUT4 availability at the surface of responsive muscle and adipose cells and influences several metabolic pathways. It also suppresses hepatic glucose production. These separate effects mean that a fall in circulating glucose cannot automatically be attributed to increased uptake alone.'],
    ['Support fasting supply','Glucagon acts strongly on liver pathways that mobilize glycogen and support glucose synthesis from precursors. Stored glycogen can be depleted, and synthesis requires substrates and energy. Regulation changes the movement of existing matter; no endocrine signal creates glucose carbon from nothing.']
  ],misconception:['Insulin is a key that every body cell needs to take in glucose.','Insulin regulates GLUT4-mediated uptake in important tissues, but other glucose transporters support uptake with different regulation. Insulin also changes liver metabolism, making a universal lock-and-key picture incomplete for explaining whole-body glucose balance.'],investigation:{title:'Account for glucose entry and use',context:'Idealized synthetic glucose-pool flows over one interval; all entries use the same arbitrary mass units and ignore distribution delays.',columns:['State','Glucose entering pool','Glucose leaving pool'],rows:[['A','12','8'],['B','8','8'],['C','6','9']],question:'What is the net pool change in each state, and can those flows identify a hormone concentration?',hint:'Subtract removal from entry, then separate a flow measurement from its possible causes.',answer:'The net changes are +4, zero and −3 units. These balances show accumulation or depletion but cannot uniquely identify insulin or glucagon concentrations.',reasoning:['State A adds twelve units and removes eight, leaving four.','State B balances flows while state C removes three more than enters.','Several mechanisms can produce the same whole-pool balance, including altered sensitivity.'],limitation:'This invented balance is not a blood-glucose simulator and omits volume changes, timing and other regulatory hormones.'},transfer:['Why can fasting glucose remain available after a meal has been absorbed?','Distinguish current intestinal input from liver stores and precursor conversion.','The liver can release glucose through glycogen breakdown and gluconeogenesis under hormonal regulation. Sustained supply depends on stores and precursors, so it is not an unlimited reserve.']});

  add({id:'kidney',title:'Kidney',category:'human-physiology',summary:'Connect renal anatomy to selective excretion, blood supply and internal balance.',aliases:['renal cortex','renal medulla','urinary system'],relatedTopics:['nephron','osmoregulation','homeostasis'],prerequisites:['heart-circulation'],sources:['es-kidney','es-nephron']},[
    'Kidneys process blood plasma and adjust what leaves the body in urine. They remove some wastes while retaining useful substances and managing water and ions. Urine then travels through ureters to the bladder; it is not stored inside the kidney.',
    'A kidney does more than strain out dirt. It first filters fluid, then selectively moves substances between tubules and blood. This two-way editing lets the body conserve valuable material while changing what is finally excreted.',
    'Locate cortex outside medulla, then trace a nephron and its associated blood vessels. Follow collecting ducts toward the renal pelvis and ureter. Use separate paths for blood and tubular fluid; their proximity does not mean their contents mix freely.',
    'Renal arteries supply blood that reaches glomerular capillaries. Filtrate enters renal tubules, where reabsorption and secretion alter its composition. Collecting ducts drain toward the calyces and pelvis. Kidneys also contribute to acid-base regulation and endocrine functions.',
    'Renal blood flow, glomerular filtration and final urine flow are distinct quantities. Much filtered water returns to circulation, so a large filtration rate can coexist with a small urine flow. Spatial organization of loops and vessels helps maintain medullary concentration gradients.',
    'Comparing urine volume alone cannot reveal every aspect of kidney function. A teaching model should also track solute amounts and concentrations. The concepts here explain system organization and are not a diagnostic interpretation of someone’s laboratory results.'
  ],[['Cortex','The outer kidney region containing renal corpuscles and many convoluted tubules.'],['Medulla','The inner region organized into pyramids containing loops and collecting ducts.'],['Ureter','A tube carrying urine from a kidney toward the bladder.']],[['Supply','Blood enters through the renal circulation.'],['Filter','Glomerular filtration begins tubular fluid processing.'],['Adjust','Tubules and collecting ducts change water and solute content.'],['Drain','Urine passes through the pelvis and ureter toward the bladder.']],[
    ['Why is filtered volume usually much greater than urine volume?',['Much filtered water is reabsorbed','Filtration means blood cells become urine','Urine is stored indefinitely in nephrons'],0,'Tubular reabsorption returns much of the filtered water to the circulation.'],
    ['Which route carries urine from a kidney to the bladder?',['Ureter','Renal artery','Renal vein'],0,'The ureter carries urine, whereas renal arteries and veins carry blood.'],
    ['Which comparison correctly distinguishes two kidney processes?',['Filtration enters tubules; reabsorption returns material toward blood','Filtration and urine excretion are identical flows','Reabsorption moves every solute into urine'],0,'Tubular editing makes final excretion different from the initial glomerular filtrate.']
  ],{curiosity:'Why filter useful substances and then recover them?',objectives:['Trace blood and urine through separate renal routes.','Relate cortex and medulla to nephron organization.','Distinguish filtration, reabsorption and excretion quantitatively.'],mechanism:[
    ['Bring plasma to the filtering interface','Renal circulation delivers blood to specialized glomerular capillaries in renal corpuscles. Pressure and barrier properties determine fluid entry into the capsule. The resulting filtrate is not whole blood: cells and most large plasma proteins are normally retained by the filtering barrier.'],
    ['Edit tubular fluid','Tubular epithelia move selected substances between the lumen and surrounding tissues. Reabsorption recovers material from filtrate, while secretion adds some substances to the tubular fluid. These routes allow final urine composition to differ markedly from the fluid that initially crossed the glomerular barrier.'],
    ['Integrate and drain','Loops, collecting ducts and local vessels support water and solute regulation within an organized cortex and medulla. Final fluid drains to the renal pelvis and ureter rather than back into an artery. Urine storage occurs primarily in the bladder, separate from this processing system.']
  ],misconception:['Everything filtered by a kidney is immediately lost in urine.','Filtration is an early processing step, not the final excreted output. Water and many useful solutes are reabsorbed, while secretion can add substances later. Measuring filtration and measuring urine therefore answer different questions.'],investigation:{title:'Separate filtered and excreted water',context:'Synthetic one-interval water accounting in a simplified kidney model; secretion of water is ignored.',columns:['Model','Filtered water (mL)','Reabsorbed water (mL)'],rows:[['A','100','99'],['B','100','97'],['C','80','79']],question:'How much water is excreted in each model, and why does equal urine output not prove equal filtration?',hint:'Subtract reabsorbed volume from filtered volume for each row.',answer:'A, B and C excrete one, three and one milliliters respectively. A and C have equal output despite different filtered volumes, because their reabsorption amounts also differ.',reasoning:['For A, one hundred minus ninety-nine leaves one milliliter.','For B and C, the same subtraction gives three and one.','Final output alone does not reveal the two larger opposing flows.'],limitation:'These invented volumes illustrate accounting only; they are not normal reference values or a renal diagnostic test.'},transfer:['Can a kidney change urine concentration without producing more waste molecules?','Consider the amount of water accompanying a fixed solute amount.','Yes. Changing water reabsorption alters the volume carrying a given solute amount, and therefore its concentration. Solute excretion and urine concentration must be compared separately.']});

  add({id:'nephron',title:'Nephron',category:'human-physiology',summary:'Follow filtration, selective transport and regulated water recovery through a nephron.',aliases:['glomerulus','Bowman capsule','loop of Henle','renal tubule'],relatedTopics:['kidney','active-transport','osmoregulation'],prerequisites:['membrane-transport','kidney'],sources:['es-nephron','es-water']},[
    'A nephron begins with a filtering structure and continues through a long tubule. Different segments recover different substances or add them to the fluid. Collecting ducts then help determine the final water content of urine.',
    'Selective transport matters more than a tube’s shape alone. Adjacent nephron segments can let water through differently or move different ions. Sending fluid through these distinct environments makes controlled recovery possible instead of one indiscriminate filter.',
    'Trace capsule, proximal tubule, descending and ascending loop limbs, distal tubule and collecting duct. Add arrows for reabsorption and secretion. Keep the capillary route beside the tubular route so movement between them remains explicit.',
    'The glomerular barrier filters plasma water and small solutes. The proximal tubule recovers much water and solute. Descending loop segments are relatively water-permeable, while the thick ascending limb moves salt without accompanying water. Distal segments and collecting ducts provide regulated adjustments.',
    'Countercurrent multiplication depends on segment-specific transport and flow; vasa recta exchange helps preserve the medullary gradient. ADH increases collecting-duct water permeability through aquaporin trafficking. Filtered load depends on filtration rate and plasma concentration, while transport capacity can limit reabsorption.',
    'A nephron diagram becomes useful when it predicts a consequence of changing a transporter or permeability. Merely labeling the loop cannot explain dilute versus concentrated urine. Predictions require a working gradient, water access and the appropriate hormonal state.'
  ],[['Filtered load','The amount of a substance filtered per unit time.'],['Reabsorption','Movement from tubular fluid back toward the circulation.'],['Secretion','Movement from blood or surrounding tissues into tubular fluid.']],[['Filter','Small-solute fluid enters the capsule.'],['Recover','Proximal transport returns much useful material.'],['Separate salt and water','Loop segments have contrasting transport properties.'],['Fine-tune','Distal pathways and collecting ducts adjust final output.']],[
    ['What distinguishes the thick ascending limb of the nephron loop?',['Salt reabsorption with low water permeability','Equal permeability to every plasma protein','Water secretion without solute transport'],0,'Salt transport without matching water movement supports the medullary concentration mechanism.'],
    ['What directly raises collecting-duct water permeability in response to ADH?',['Increased apical aquaporin availability','Conversion of water into sodium','Removal of every epithelial membrane'],0,'ADH promotes aquaporin insertion, allowing more water movement when a gradient exists.'],
    ['Which equation correctly accounts for solute excretion?',['Filtered − reabsorbed + secreted','Filtered + reabsorbed − secreted','Reabsorbed − filtered − secreted'],0,'Recovery lowers tubular amount, while secretion adds to the amount eventually excreted.']
  ],{curiosity:'How can neighboring tubules treat water differently?',objectives:['Explain different transport roles along the nephron.','Calculate solute excretion from three component flows.','Connect ADH response to an existing osmotic gradient.'],mechanism:[
    ['Form a selective filtrate','Blood pressure supports filtration through the glomerular barrier into the capsule. The composition entering the tubule depends on size, charge and barrier integrity rather than on whether a molecule is useful or waste. Useful small solutes therefore commonly need recovery afterward.'],
    ['Recover through specialized segments','The proximal tubule reabsorbs many solutes and much water, whereas loop segments create contrasting salt and water movements. The thick ascending limb reabsorbs salt with little accompanying water. This contrast helps establish a gradient but does not by itself specify the final urine concentration.'],
    ['Regulate final water recovery','In collecting-duct cells, ADH can increase apical aquaporin availability and therefore water permeability. Water then moves according to the local osmotic gradient. A permeability change and a driving gradient are both needed; a hormone does not actively pump water molecules through a channel.']
  ],misconception:['ADH pumps water out of the collecting duct.','ADH changes the availability of water channels, increasing permeability. Water moves passively according to its gradient. The medullary environment and tubular conditions therefore matter as well as the hormone signal when predicting water recovery.'],investigation:{title:'Balance a filtered solute',context:'Synthetic solute accounting per minute in three nephron models; all columns use the same arbitrary amount units.',columns:['Model','Filtered','Reabsorbed','Secreted'],rows:[['A','10','7','1'],['B','10','9','0'],['C','8','3','2']],question:'Which model excretes the most solute, and why is the highest filtered amount insufficient to answer?',hint:'For each row calculate filtered minus reabsorbed plus secreted.',answer:'C excretes seven units per minute, compared with four for A and one for B. Its lower filtered amount is outweighed by lower recovery and added secretion.',reasoning:['A gives ten minus seven plus one, or four units.','B gives one unit while C gives eight minus three plus two.','All three processes must be considered before comparing final excretion.'],limitation:'The invented flows omit concentration dependence, transporter saturation and interactions between segments of a real nephron.'},transfer:['Would adding more aquaporins guarantee concentrated urine if the medullary gradient disappeared?','Separate membrane permeability from the force driving net water movement.','No. More channels increase permeability but cannot create the missing osmotic gradient. Without a suitable driving difference, increased permeability alone cannot produce the usual water recovery.']});

  add({id:'osmoregulation',title:'Osmoregulation',category:'human-physiology',summary:'Separate water balance, solute amount and the hormonal control of fluid concentration.',aliases:['ADH','vasopressin','water balance','osmolality'],relatedTopics:['nephron','osmosis','endocrine-system'],prerequisites:['osmosis','homeostasis'],sources:['es-water','es-nephron']},[
    'Osmoregulation keeps body fluids within a useful range of concentration. Water intake, water loss and solute handling all matter. In humans, thirst and kidney responses coordinate this balance rather than keeping every fluid volume perfectly fixed.',
    'Adding water and adding salt are not equivalent changes. Concentration depends on their ratio, while total fluid volume is a separate property. A regulatory system must therefore track more than the number of water molecules alone.',
    'Compare a body-fluid compartment before water loss, after water loss and after regulated recovery. Follow an ADH signal to collecting-duct water channels. The diagram distinguishes an altered concentration from an altered total solute amount.',
    'Hypothalamic osmoreceptors respond to changing plasma concentration. ADH is synthesized in hypothalamic neurons and released from the posterior pituitary; it can raise collecting-duct water permeability. Thirst increases potential intake. Volume and pressure signals also influence these responses.',
    'Osmolality counts dissolved particles per kilogram of solvent; tonicity concerns effects of effectively nonpenetrating solutes on cell volume. ADH-mediated water recovery requires a suitable medullary gradient. Regulation of sodium content and extracellular volume overlaps with, but is not identical to, osmoregulation.',
    'A concentration measurement without a volume or solute account can be misleading. Classroom models should specify which solutes cross a membrane and whether pressure can change. These mechanisms do not provide personalized hydration targets or medical interpretation.'
  ],[['Osmolality','The number of dissolved osmotic particles per kilogram of solvent.'],['ADH','A hormone also called vasopressin that helps regulate renal water recovery.'],['Osmoreceptor','A sensory cell responsive to changes in fluid osmotic concentration.']],[['Disturb','Water loss can raise concentration without adding solute.'],['Sense','Osmotic and circulatory signals inform regulatory centers.'],['Respond','Thirst and renal permeability influence water balance.'],['Reassess','Changing fluid conditions modify the continuing response.']],[
    ['What happens to concentration if water leaves but solute amount stays fixed?',['Concentration rises','Concentration must fall','Solute atoms disappear'],0,'The same solute amount occupies less solvent, raising its concentration.'],
    ['Where is ADH synthesized before posterior-pituitary release?',['Hypothalamic neurons','Red blood cells','The bladder wall'],0,'Hypothalamic neurons make ADH, which is transported for storage and posterior-pituitary release.'],
    ['Why should osmotic concentration and fluid volume be distinguished?',['Equal concentrations can occur at different total volumes','They are always numerically identical','Concentration cannot change without new salt'],0,'Concentration is a ratio, so it does not uniquely specify total water or solute amount.']
  ],{curiosity:'Is fluid concentration the same as fluid volume?',objectives:['Predict concentration changes from water and solute accounting.','Trace ADH from synthesis to renal response.','Distinguish osmotic regulation from total volume regulation.'],mechanism:[
    ['Create a concentration disturbance','When water is lost without a proportional solute loss, the remaining fluid becomes more concentrated. Cells may then lose water depending on membrane permeability. The same concentration change could arise from solute addition, so concentration alone does not reveal the initiating event.'],
    ['Integrate the sensed signals','Hypothalamic osmoreceptors contribute to thirst and ADH release, while circulatory volume and pressure inputs also affect regulation. ADH is synthesized in hypothalamic neurons before release from the posterior pituitary. These overlapping inputs prevent a one-variable diagram from describing every physiological circumstance.'],
    ['Adjust access to water','ADH increases collecting-duct water permeability, allowing greater recovery where a suitable osmotic gradient exists. Thirst can support replacement through intake. Feedback changes as the disturbance is corrected, but the system cannot recover water that is unavailable or compensate indefinitely for continuing losses.']
  ],misconception:['ADH creates extra water when the body is short of it.','The hormone changes how much existing water is recovered from tubular fluid. It does not manufacture water. Conservation can reduce further loss, while replacing a deficit also depends on available intake and the continuing balance of losses.'],investigation:{title:'A ratio can change without added solute',context:'Idealized synthetic compartments containing a fixed amount of nonpenetrating solute; pressure and volume interactions are omitted.',columns:['State','Solute amount (units)','Water volume (L)'],rows:[['Initial','30','3'],['Water loss','30','2'],['Water restored','30','3']],question:'What is the concentration after water loss, and how much has it increased relative to the initial state?',hint:'Divide solute amount by water volume and compare the resulting ratios.',answer:'Concentration rises from ten to fifteen units per liter, a 50% increase. No solute was added; the change results entirely from the smaller modeled water volume.',reasoning:['Initially thirty solute units divided by three liters gives ten.','After water loss thirty divided by two gives fifteen units per liter.','The increase of five is half the initial concentration of ten.'],limitation:'This simple invented compartment omits permeating solutes, multiple fluid spaces and pressure effects in an actual body.'},transfer:['Can two compartments have equal osmotic concentration but different total solute amounts?','Compare ratios while allowing the water volumes to differ.','Yes. A larger compartment can contain proportionally more water and more solute while retaining the same concentration. Equal concentration therefore does not imply equal amounts or volumes.']});

  add({id:'muscle-contraction',title:'Muscle Contraction',category:'human-physiology',summary:'Link excitation, calcium regulation and ATP-driven sliding in skeletal muscle.',aliases:['sliding filament','sarcomere','actin myosin','cross bridge'],relatedTopics:['action-potential','atp','cellular-respiration'],prerequisites:['atp','action-potential'],sources:['es-muscle']},[
    'Skeletal muscle generates force when tiny molecular motors repeatedly pull on neighboring filaments. The filaments slide rather than becoming shorter themselves. A nerve signal, calcium release and a continuing ATP supply coordinate this process.',
    'Producing tension does not guarantee visible shortening. Motors can pull while an opposing load holds muscle length steady. The same molecular mechanism can therefore support movement or maintain posture, depending on the forces acting on the muscle.',
    'Follow electrical excitation into a muscle fiber, calcium release beside its filaments, and repeated myosin attachment cycles. Compare sarcomere boundaries before and after shortening while keeping individual thick and thin filament lengths unchanged.',
    'In skeletal muscle, calcium binding to troponin changes tropomyosin position and permits actin-myosin interaction. ATP binding releases myosin from actin, and ATP hydrolysis prepares another cycle. Calcium pumping back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum supports relaxation.',
    'Force depends on activation, filament overlap and mechanical load, not simply ATP concentration. Isometric contraction generates tension without overall shortening. ATP supports both cross-bridge cycling and calcium removal; without ATP, normal detachment and active relaxation cannot continue.',
    'A force trace and a length trace answer different questions in a muscle experiment. Comparing both distinguishes force development from shortening. The lesson describes a skeletal-muscle mechanism and should not be transferred unchanged to smooth or cardiac muscle regulation.'
  ],[['Sarcomere','A repeating contractile unit between adjacent Z discs in striated muscle.'],['Cross-bridge','An interaction between a myosin head and actin during force generation.'],['Isometric','Producing force without changing overall length in the stated mechanical condition.']],[['Excite','A signal triggers calcium release from intracellular stores.'],['Expose','Calcium regulation permits myosin to interact with actin.'],['Cycle','ATP-dependent attachments slide filaments and generate force.'],['Relax','Calcium recovery reduces activation when the signal ends.']],[
    ['What directly allows a bound myosin head to detach from actin?',['ATP binding','Complete absence of ATP','Shortening of the actin molecule'],0,'ATP binding lowers myosin affinity for actin and permits detachment in the cycle.'],
    ['What changes during sarcomere shortening?',['Overlap increases as filaments slide','Each thick filament loses half its length','Actin becomes a different chemical element'],0,'Filaments slide past one another while their individual lengths remain essentially unchanged.'],
    ['Which observation describes an isometric contraction?',['Tension rises while overall length stays fixed','Length decreases with no force production','Myosin cannot interact with actin'],0,'Isometric conditions constrain length even while activated muscle develops tension.']
  ],{curiosity:'Can a muscle work without visibly shortening?',objectives:['Trace excitation through calcium to filament interaction.','Explain ATP requirements for cycling and relaxation.','Distinguish tension from shortening using paired measurements.'],mechanism:[
    ['Expose the interaction sites','An action potential in a skeletal muscle fiber triggers calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Calcium binds troponin, altering the regulatory arrangement on thin filaments. This permits cross-bridge interaction but does not make the filaments spontaneously shorten as isolated protein strands.'],
    ['Cycle rather than lock','Myosin heads bind actin and generate force through changing conformations. Fresh ATP binding allows detachment, and hydrolysis supports resetting for another cycle. Coordinated cycling across many heads permits continued sliding rather than one permanent attachment that could perform only a single pull.'],
    ['Balance activation with mechanics','Observed shortening depends on load and available force. Under fixed-length conditions, tension can rise without whole-muscle shortening. When excitation ends, ATP-dependent calcium pumps help restore low cytosolic calcium, reducing activation; relaxation therefore requires active cellular processes rather than simple exhaustion of ATP.']
  ],misconception:['Muscle relaxes automatically as soon as it runs out of ATP.','ATP is needed for myosin detachment and calcium recovery as well as repeated force-generating cycles. Severe ATP depletion disrupts these processes and can leave strong attachments, so energy exhaustion is not the normal mechanism of relaxation.'],investigation:{title:'Force without shortening',context:'Synthetic readings from activated muscle models with different external constraints; force and length are illustrative, not training measurements.',columns:['Condition','Length change (mm)','Force increase (units)'],rows:[['Unstimulated control','0','0'],['Stimulated, fixed length','0','8'],['Stimulated, movable load','−3','5']],question:'Which stimulated condition is isometric, and what does it show about equating contraction with shortening?',hint:'Find force production without a change in the measured length.',answer:'The fixed-length preparation is isometric: force increases by eight units with zero length change. Muscle activation and force generation do not require observable overall shortening.',reasoning:['The unstimulated control shows neither additional force nor a length change.','The fixed-length model develops force despite an unchanged external length.','The movable-load model demonstrates that a different constraint permits shortening.'],limitation:'The fictional readings omit series elasticity, recruitment and detailed force-length relationships within real muscle fibers.'},transfer:['Why would blocking calcium reuptake interfere with relaxation even if ATP remains available for myosin?','Consider whether the thin-filament regulatory signal has actually ended.','Cytosolic calcium could remain elevated and continue permitting actin-myosin interaction. ATP availability alone does not switch off activation; restoring calcium distribution is a separate requirement.']});

  add({id:'reproductive-anatomy',title:'Reproductive Anatomy',category:'human-physiology',summary:'Connect gonads, ducts and support tissues without confusing production, transport and development.',aliases:['testis','ovary','uterus','oviduct','epididymis'],relatedTopics:['gametogenesis','fertilization','endocrine-system'],prerequisites:['cell-structure'],sources:['es-reproduction']},[
    'Human reproductive structures have different jobs: producing gametes, supporting their maturation, transporting them and supporting development. Testes produce sperm; ovaries contain developing oocytes. The uterus supports implantation and pregnancy, rather than manufacturing eggs.',
    'Knowing where a cell is produced does not tell you its entire journey. Production, maturation and transport can occur in different structures. Following that route explains why an intact gonad alone does not guarantee that gametes can meet.',
    'Trace the sperm route from seminiferous tubules through epididymis and ducts. Separately trace an ovulated oocyte toward a uterine tube and the uterus. This schematic separates pathways and labels common anatomy without treating every body as identical.',
    'Sertoli cells support germ-cell development within seminiferous tubules, while Leydig cells produce testosterone. Ovarian follicles support oocyte development. Uterine tubes participate in transport and are the usual site of fertilization; the endometrium is the uterine lining involved in implantation.',
    'Gonads combine gamete-related and endocrine functions, but those functions use distinct cell populations and can be affected differently. Duct patency, ciliary movement and smooth-muscle activity contribute to transport. Anatomical variation and differences of sex development mean a single diagram cannot describe everyone.',
    'Precise anatomical language makes biological communication clearer without assigning gender identity, behavior or fertility from one organ. This lesson is educational, not advice about conception, contraception, pregnancy or personal symptoms.'
  ],[['Gonad','An organ associated with gamete development and reproductive hormone production.'],['Epididymis','A coiled duct involved in sperm maturation, storage and transport.'],['Endometrium','The inner uterine lining that changes with hormonal conditions.']],[['Develop','Gonads support germ cells and produce hormones.'],['Mature','Specialized supporting cells and ducts contribute to gamete function.'],['Transport','Distinct ducts and uterine tubes move reproductive cells.'],['Support','The uterus provides a site for implantation and later development.']],[
    ['Which structure is the usual site of human fertilization?',['A uterine tube','The urinary bladder','The endometrium itself'],0,'Fertilization usually occurs in a uterine tube, while implantation later involves the endometrium.'],
    ['Which statement correctly distinguishes production from maturation?',['Sperm develop in testes and undergo further maturation in the epididymis','The uterus produces sperm and oocytes','Sperm are made only in the prostate'],0,'Different structures contribute to sperm production, maturation and subsequent transport.'],
    ['Why can gonadal hormone secretion persist despite impaired gamete transport?',['Hormone-producing cells and transport pathways have different roles','Hormones always travel inside gametes','Blocked ducts remove every endocrine cell'],0,'A transport obstruction need not eliminate the separate cells responsible for endocrine secretion.']
  ],{curiosity:'Why trace routes instead of memorizing organ names?',objectives:['Distinguish gamete production, maturation and transport.','Locate fertilization and implantation as separate events.','Explain why anatomy does not determine an individual identity or reproductive outcome.'],mechanism:[
    ['Assign roles to tissues','Gonads contain specialized cells that support gamete development and other cells that contribute hormones. These functions interact without being identical. A diagram that labels an entire organ with a single job can obscure how its different cell populations cooperate or respond differently to disruption.'],
    ['Follow the transport pathway','After production, sperm pass through a maturation and duct system. An ovulated oocyte is taken toward a uterine tube through a different route. Ciliary activity, fluid conditions and muscular movements matter, so a drawn connection does not imply passive movement through an empty pipe.'],
    ['Separate meeting from implantation','Human fertilization usually takes place in a uterine tube. Early development and transport precede implantation in the uterine endometrium. These distinct locations explain why fertilization and uterine implantation should not be merged into one event or assumed to occur at the same moment.']
  ],misconception:['The uterus produces eggs, so fertilization and implantation happen there together.','Oocytes develop in ovaries and fertilization usually occurs in a uterine tube. Implantation is a later interaction with the uterine lining. Keeping production, fertilization and implantation separate prevents both anatomical and timing errors.'],investigation:{title:'Production is not delivery',context:'Fictional labeled-cell tracking in three simplified reproductive transport models; these are conceptual observations, not human tests.',columns:['Model','Cells produced','Cells detected after duct'],rows:[['Connected route','20','16'],['Interrupted route','20','0'],['No production control','0','0']],question:'Which result separates production from delivery, and why can the tracking data not establish hormone secretion?',hint:'Compare the matched production counts before reasoning about a separate tissue function.',answer:'The interrupted route produces twenty cells but delivers none to the sampled downstream location. Tracking cells measures transport, not the output of hormone-producing cells.',reasoning:['Both production-positive models start with the same number of labeled cells.','Only the connected route has cells detected beyond the duct.','Endocrine output requires a separate measurement rather than inference from cell delivery.'],limitation:'This invented route model omits individual anatomical variation, physiological feedback and the many determinants of human fertility.'},transfer:['Would locating an ovary or testis alone establish whether a person can produce offspring?','Distinguish one structure from the complete sequence of reproductive functions.','No. Anatomy alone cannot establish gamete function, transport, fertilization or developmental support. Individual outcomes depend on multiple biological factors, and an educational diagram cannot assess a person.']});

  add({id:'gametogenesis',title:'Gametogenesis',category:'human-physiology',summary:'Compare chromosome reduction and cellular specialization during sperm and oocyte development.',aliases:['spermatogenesis','oogenesis','spermatid','polar body'],relatedTopics:['meiosis','reproductive-anatomy','fertilization'],prerequisites:['meiosis','cell-differentiation'],sources:['es-reproduction','es-gametes']},[
    'Gametes carry one chromosome set instead of the usual two. Producing them requires meiosis and specialized development. Human sperm production and oocyte development use different timing and unequal divisions, so they do not both yield four equivalent gametes.',
    'Reducing chromosome sets and building a specialized cell are different problems. Meiosis distributes genetic material; later or overlapping development builds the structures and stored resources a gamete needs. A haploid cell is not automatically a fully functional gamete.',
    'Follow one replicated primary cell through two meiotic divisions. Compare four spermatids with one large oocyte lineage and smaller polar bodies. Count chromosome sets separately from DNA amount, and mark the meiotic arrests in the oocyte pathway.',
    'Spermatogenesis yields four haploid spermatids from one primary spermatocyte under ideal completion; differentiation then forms sperm. Human primary oocytes arrest in prophase I, and secondary oocytes normally arrest in metaphase II until activation associated with fertilization permits completion.',
    'Oocyte cytokinesis is asymmetric, preserving most cytoplasm in one developing gamete. Crossing-over and chromosome assortment generate variation, while chromosome segregation errors can alter chromosome number. Cellular maturation, meiotic progression and hormonal coordination are related but distinct controls.',
    'Chromosome diagrams help explain inheritance only when their accounting boundary is clear. Four meiotic products do not mean four viable children, and an ideal cell-output calculation is not a prediction of fertility or an individual’s reproductive timeline.'
  ],[['Gametogenesis','The processes that produce and specialize reproductive cells.'],['Spermatid','A haploid product of spermatocyte meiosis before final sperm differentiation.'],['Polar body','A small cell formed by an asymmetric oocyte meiotic division.']],[['Prepare','A primary meiotic cell has replicated chromosomes.'],['Reduce','Meiosis I separates homologous chromosome sets.'],['Separate','Meiosis II separates sister chromatids.'],['Specialize','Unequal cytokinesis or sperm differentiation produces different gamete forms.']],[
    ['What is the ideal meiotic output from one primary spermatocyte?',['Four haploid spermatids','Four diploid oocytes','Two genetically identical diploid cells'],0,'Two meiotic divisions can form four haploid spermatids before further sperm differentiation.'],
    ['Why does oogenesis not produce four equivalent large eggs?',['Cytoplasm is partitioned asymmetrically into an oocyte lineage and polar bodies','Oocytes never undergo meiosis','Every meiotic product has a full diploid chromosome set'],0,'Asymmetric divisions preserve most cytoplasm in one developing gamete rather than distributing it equally.'],
    ['What normally describes an ovulated human secondary oocyte?',['Haploid chromosome sets with sister chromatids still paired before meiosis II completion','A mature diploid embryo','A cell that has never replicated DNA'],0,'The secondary oocyte has completed meiosis I but normally awaits completion of meiosis II.']
  ],{curiosity:'Is a haploid cell already a finished gamete?',objectives:['Count meiotic products without confusing them with offspring.','Compare symmetric and asymmetric germ-cell divisions.','Distinguish chromosome sets, DNA replication and maturation.'],mechanism:[
    ['Reduce sets after replication','A primary meiotic cell replicates DNA before the two divisions. Meiosis I separates homologs, reducing chromosome sets, while meiosis II separates sister chromatids. These distinctions let a cell be haploid even when each chromosome still contains two chromatids after the first division.'],
    ['Partition cells differently','Spermatocyte meiosis can yield four haploid spermatids. Oocyte divisions instead allocate most cytoplasm to one large cell and much less to polar bodies. Equal chromosome segregation therefore does not imply equal cell size, equal cytoplasmic resources or four equally functional gametes.'],
    ['Complete specialization and timing','Spermatids undergo structural differentiation, and oocyte progression includes prolonged meiotic arrests. In humans the secondary oocyte normally completes meiosis II after fertilization-associated activation. Completion of a chromosome division is only one component of maturation and does not by itself establish developmental competence.']
  ],misconception:['Meiosis always produces four equally functional reproductive cells.','Four haploid products can be a useful chromosome-accounting model, but their cell sizes and developmental roles differ. Oocyte divisions are asymmetric, and even spermatids need further differentiation before they become mature sperm cells.'],investigation:{title:'Count products, not offspring',context:'Idealized theoretical completion of spermatocyte meiosis; assume no cell loss and do not infer reproductive success.',columns:['Primary spermatocytes','After meiosis I cells','Final spermatids'],rows:[['1','2','4'],['3','6','12'],['5','10','20']],question:'How many spermatids result from five starting cells, and why is that number not a count of mature sperm or offspring?',hint:'Follow both meiotic divisions, then consider events outside that calculation.',answer:'Twenty spermatids result under the stated assumptions. Differentiation, survival and later reproductive events are outside this count, so it predicts neither mature functional sperm nor offspring.',reasoning:['Each primary spermatocyte produces two cells in the first modeled division.','A second division doubles those products again to four per starting cell.','Maturation and successful reproduction require additional processes not counted in the table.'],limitation:'The idealized count excludes cell loss, segregation errors and biological variation during real gamete development.'},transfer:['After meiosis I, why can a cell be haploid even while each chromosome still has two chromatids?','Define ploidy using homologous chromosome sets rather than DNA strands.','Haploid describes one member of each homologous pair. Sister chromatids remain copies of that chromosome, so their presence increases DNA amount without restoring a second homologous set.']});

  add({id:'fertilization',title:'Fertilization',category:'human-physiology',summary:'Connect gamete fusion, egg activation and chromosome-set restoration in a mammalian example.',aliases:['zygote','egg activation','polyspermy','cortical reaction'],relatedTopics:['gametogenesis','meiosis','cell-cycle'],prerequisites:['meiosis','cell-membrane'],sources:['es-fertilization','es-gametes']},[
    'Fertilization combines genetic contributions from two gametes and starts a new developmental sequence. It includes recognition, membrane fusion and activation of the oocyte. It is not the same event as later implantation in the uterus.',
    'Delivering chromosomes is only part of the transition. The oocyte must also switch its cellular program and resist additional sperm fusion. Genetic combination and cellular activation cooperate, but measuring one does not prove that every later developmental step succeeded.',
    'Trace sperm-oocyte contact, membrane fusion, calcium-linked activation and formation of parental pronuclei. Mark the completion of oocyte meiosis and the first cleavage separately. The sequence is a mammalian schematic rather than a universal pattern for every animal.',
    'Mammalian sperm undergo capacitation and acrosomal changes associated with fertilization. Fusion triggers calcium signaling in the oocyte, promoting cortical-granule release, completion of meiosis II and other activation events. Changes to egg coats and the membrane help limit polyspermy.',
    'Polyspermy prevention differs among animal groups; a sea-urchin electrical fast block should not be assumed to explain mammalian fertilization. Parental chromosomes are organized in pronuclei before their participation in the first mitotic division. Successful fusion alone does not guarantee continued embryo development.',
    'Comparing fusion, activation and cleavage as separate experimental outcomes prevents a single positive marker from being called complete reproductive success. This is a conceptual lesson, not a procedure for assisted reproduction or personal pregnancy guidance.'
  ],[['Zygote','The cell resulting from fertilization before early embryonic divisions.'],['Polyspermy','Entry or fusion of more than one sperm in a context requiring a single sperm contribution.'],['Cortical reaction','Release of oocyte cortical-granule contents following activation.']],[['Contact','Gametes interact through regulated surface and coat processes.'],['Fuse','Their plasma membranes fuse and sperm contents enter.'],['Activate','Calcium signaling coordinates several oocyte responses.'],['Begin development','Chromosome contributions are organized before early cleavage.']],[
    ['Why must fertilization be distinguished from implantation?',['Fusion and activation precede later attachment to uterine tissue','They are identical events with different names','Implantation produces the sperm nucleus'],0,'Fertilization and implantation involve distinct mechanisms, locations and developmental times.'],
    ['What does cortical-granule release contribute to after mammalian egg activation?',['Changes that help reduce additional sperm interaction','Production of a new ovary','Removal of every maternal chromosome'],0,'Released granule contents modify the extracellular environment and contribute to polyspermy prevention.'],
    ['What is restored when two typical haploid human gamete contributions combine?',['A diploid chromosome-set complement','Four complete parental chromosome sets','Only the maternal chromosomes'],0,'Combining one haploid contribution from each gamete restores the usual diploid complement.']
  ],{curiosity:'Why is chromosome delivery only part of fertilization?',objectives:['Separate fusion, activation and implantation.','Explain why additional sperm entry must be limited.','Distinguish a measured early event from later developmental success.'],mechanism:[
    ['Reach a compatible interface','Gametes must reach each other and interact through regulated surface processes before fusion occurs. Mammalian sperm undergo capacitation and acrosomal changes, while the oocyte is surrounded by extracellular structures. A simple drawing of a sperm touching an egg omits these important prerequisites.'],
    ['Activate the oocyte','Fusion-associated calcium signals coordinate completion of oocyte meiosis and cortical-granule secretion alongside other cellular changes. These events convert an arrested gamete state into an activated developmental program. The signal and the downstream responses can be measured separately, which is useful when assessing a proposed mechanism.'],
    ['Coordinate contributions and progression','Parental genetic contributions are organized in pronuclei before the first mitotic division. Mechanisms that limit additional sperm interaction protect the intended chromosome complement. Successful early activation is not equivalent to implantation or a viable pregnancy, because many later developmental and tissue interactions remain necessary.']
  ],misconception:['Seeing sperm entry proves that all later development will succeed.','Entry is one early event. Oocyte activation, chromosome organization, cleavage, implantation and subsequent development require additional processes. An experiment must state which outcome it measures rather than treating one positive marker as proof of complete reproductive success.'],investigation:{title:'Different outcomes at different stages',context:'Synthetic counts from equal groups of 100 conceptual mammalian oocytes; categories are sequential observations, not clinical success rates.',columns:['Model','Fusion detected','Activation detected','First cleavage detected'],rows:[['A','80','72','60'],['B','80','40','25'],['C','0','0','0']],question:'Which model shows a larger loss between fusion and activation, and what should not be concluded from the cleavage count?',hint:'Subtract adjacent-stage counts and keep later developmental outcomes outside the evidence.',answer:'B loses forty detections between fusion and activation, compared with eight in A. First cleavage counts do not establish implantation, pregnancy or later developmental success.',reasoning:['Both models begin with eighty detected fusion events, enabling this comparison.','Activation is detected in seventy-two cases for A and forty for B.','The final recorded endpoint is cleavage, not any later reproductive outcome.'],limitation:'The invented counts do not measure human treatment outcomes and cannot isolate a molecular cause without further controlled evidence.'},transfer:['Why should an electrical block observed in sea-urchin eggs not automatically explain mammalian fertilization?','Ask which mechanism has actually been demonstrated in the species being discussed.','Fertilization mechanisms vary across animal groups. A shared need to limit polyspermy does not imply the same electrical or extracellular response, so mammalian claims require mammalian evidence.']});

  add({id:'innate-immunity',title:'Innate Immunity',category:'human-physiology',summary:'Connect barriers, pattern recognition and inflammation to early defense and adaptive activation.',aliases:['phagocytosis','inflammation','pattern recognition','complement'],relatedTopics:['immunity','immune-memory','blood'],prerequisites:['cell-membrane','immunity'],sources:['es-innate','es-trained-innate']},[
    'Innate defenses include physical barriers, chemical conditions and cells that respond rapidly to danger. Some cells engulf material, while signaling molecules recruit help. These defenses act before a newly tailored adaptive response has fully developed.',
    'Fast recognition does not mean recognizing nothing in particular. Innate receptors detect recurring molecular patterns and signs of tissue damage. This broad recognition supports quick action, but the response still needs control because inflammation can also harm host tissues.',
    'Follow a barrier breach to local recognition, inflammatory signaling, recruited cells and clearance. Show a separate connection to antigen presentation and adaptive activation. Do not draw every invading microbe as automatically destroyed at the first encounter.',
    'Pattern-recognition receptors detect microbial or damage-associated signals. Phagocytes can ingest targets, complement supports several defense functions, and natural killer cells respond to altered host-cell signals. Cytokines and vascular changes coordinate inflammation and communication with adaptive immunity.',
    'Innate recognition uses germline-encoded receptors rather than the clonally rearranged antigen receptors of adaptive lymphocytes. Some innate cells can show trained responses, but this differs from classical antigen-specific B- and T-cell memory. Inflammation can occur without infection after sterile injury.',
    'An increase in an inflammatory marker is evidence of a response, not a unique identification of a pathogen. Controlled comparisons must separate recognition, recruitment and killing. This educational distinction is not a diagnostic rule for interpreting personal symptoms.'
  ],[['Pattern-recognition receptor','A receptor detecting recurring microbial or damage-associated molecular features.'],['Phagocytosis','Cellular engulfment of particles into membrane-bound compartments.'],['Inflammation','A regulated tissue response involving signaling, vascular changes and immune-cell activity.']],[['Barrier','Physical and chemical defenses reduce entry.'],['Recognize','Cells detect microbial patterns or tissue damage.'],['Recruit and act','Signals coordinate local defenses and cell arrival.'],['Resolve and connect','Regulation limits damage and links to adaptive responses.']],[
    ['Which statement best describes innate pattern recognition?',['It detects recurring molecular features and damage signals','It detects absolutely every pathogen with a unique rearranged receptor','It begins only after antibodies appear'],0,'Innate receptors recognize shared patterns without waiting for newly generated antigen-specific antibodies.'],
    ['Can inflammation occur without a microbial infection?',['Yes, sterile tissue damage can trigger it','No, every inflammatory response proves infection','Only if antibodies are absent'],0,'Damage-associated signals can induce inflammation even when no microbe initiated the injury.'],
    ['Why is more inflammation not always a better outcome?',['Excessive responses can damage host tissue','Inflammation never recruits useful defenses','Host cells are immune to every inflammatory mediator'],0,'Effective defense needs regulation because inflammatory activity can injure the tissue it protects.']
  ],{curiosity:'Does rapid recognition mean nonspecific recognition?',objectives:['Connect barriers to cellular innate responses.','Distinguish pattern recognition from adaptive receptor specificity.','Interpret inflammatory measurements without equating them with infection.'],mechanism:[
    ['Limit entry and recognize disturbance','Skin, mucosal surfaces and local chemical conditions reduce microbial entry. When a barrier is breached, cells can detect recurring microbial features or signals from damaged tissue. Recognition therefore depends on molecular interactions rather than an undefined ability to label anything unfamiliar as foreign.'],
    ['Recruit and remove','Cytokines and other mediators influence local vessels and recruit responding cells. Phagocytes can ingest material, while complement and natural killer cells add different defense mechanisms. No single pathway guarantees elimination, and each measured response describes only part of the ongoing interaction.'],
    ['Regulate and connect','Resolution mechanisms limit collateral tissue injury as danger decreases. Antigen-presenting cells can connect innate detection to adaptive activation. Although trained innate responses exist, they should not be equated with the classical antigen-specific memory built through B- and T-cell selection and differentiation.']
  ],misconception:['Inflammation proves that bacteria are present.','Inflammation can follow infection, but sterile tissue injury can also release danger signals. A response marker is not a unique cause marker, so identifying a pathogen requires additional evidence rather than an inference from inflammation alone.'],investigation:{title:'A signal does not uniquely identify its cause',context:'Fictional tissue-model readings at a matched time; the marker is an arbitrary inflammation index, not a patient test.',columns:['Condition','Inflammation index','Microbial signal detected'],rows:[['Untreated control','1','No'],['Sterile damage','7','No'],['Microbial exposure','9','Yes']],question:'Which comparison shows that inflammation is not specific to microbial exposure, and what extra conclusion remains unsupported?',hint:'Compare the damage condition with the control before interpreting the exposure condition.',answer:'Sterile damage raises the index from one to seven without a detected microbial signal. The table does not identify a particular pathogen or show that the response successfully clears it.',reasoning:['The untreated control establishes a lower baseline in this fictional model.','Sterile damage produces a strong response despite no detected microbial signal.','The exposure row cannot establish organism identity or successful clearance from one index.'],limitation:'These invented observations do not validate any diagnostic marker and omit detection limits, timing and tissue-specific responses.'},transfer:['Why could blocking inflammatory recruitment reduce both tissue damage and microbial clearance?','Consider the beneficial and harmful actions of the same arriving cells.','Recruited cells can attack microbes while also releasing substances that injure surrounding tissue. Reducing recruitment may therefore change both outcomes, so evaluating only one endpoint can hide a tradeoff.']});

  add({id:'plant-tissues',title:'Plant Tissues',category:'plant-biology',summary:'Connect meristems, tissue systems and cell-wall specialization to plant function.',aliases:['meristem','parenchyma','collenchyma','sclerenchyma','vascular tissue'],relatedTopics:['cell-wall','xylem','phloem','plant-transport'],prerequisites:['cell-structure','cell-wall'],sources:['es-tissues','es-stems']},[
    'Plant tissues group cells with related roles in growth, protection, support and transport. Meristems supply new cells. Other cells develop specialized walls and connections, allowing roots, stems and leaves to carry out different but coordinated functions.',
    'A thick wall can support a plant yet also change how its cell works. Some water-conducting cells function after losing their living contents, while sugar transport depends on living cells. Structure helps explain why not all plant tissue can be treated alike.',
    'Start with a meristem and follow cells into dermal, ground and vascular systems. Compare thin primary walls, uneven supportive walls and thick lignified walls. The process diagram shows developmental alternatives rather than a literal cross-section of every species.',
    'Dermal tissues cover surfaces, ground tissues perform functions including storage and photosynthesis, and vascular tissues distribute water or assimilates. Parenchyma, collenchyma and sclerenchyma differ in wall properties and typical roles. Apical meristems contribute primary growth; lateral meristems can support secondary growth.',
    'Xylem includes dead conducting elements but also living cells, so the whole tissue is not uniformly dead. Angiosperm sieve-tube elements remain living although they lose their nucleus and rely on associated companion cells. Tissue identity depends on differentiation and position, not wall thickness alone.',
    'Plant anatomy helps interpret why a young flexible stem differs from mature wood or a storage root. A sample section needs positional context: observing a thick wall alone cannot establish the entire tissue’s transport role or developmental origin.'
  ],[['Meristem','A region containing cells that contribute to continued plant growth through division.'],['Lignin','A wall-associated polymer contributing stiffness and other properties in many specialized plant cells.'],['Vascular tissue','Plant tissue specialized for long-distance transport, including xylem and phloem.']],[['Divide','Meristematic cells provide new cells for growth.'],['Differentiate','Position and signals guide cell specialization.'],['Construct','Walls and connections develop for particular roles.'],['Cooperate','Tissue systems integrate transport, support, protection and metabolism.']],[
    ['Which statement about xylem tissue is accurate?',['It includes dead conducting elements and some living cells','Every xylem cell must be a living sieve tube','It contains only dead cells without exceptions'],0,'Xylem is a complex tissue that includes conducting elements and living parenchyma.'],
    ['Which feature supports flexibility in many young growing organs?',['Collenchyma with unevenly thickened primary walls','A complete absence of cell walls','Every cell becoming a mature vessel element'],0,'Collenchyma provides support while its primary walls can accommodate growing tissues.'],
    ['What distinguishes a meristem from a fully specialized tissue region?',['It supplies new cells through continuing division','It can never contain living cells','It transports only oxygen through empty pipes'],0,'Meristematic regions maintain cell division that contributes to growth and subsequent differentiation.']
  ],{curiosity:'Can a cell keep working after losing its living contents?',objectives:['Distinguish meristematic and differentiated tissue roles.','Relate cell-wall properties to support and transport.','Avoid classifying a complex tissue from one cell feature alone.'],mechanism:[
    ['Supply growing tissues','Meristems generate cells that contribute to root, shoot or secondary growth. Their descendants can follow different developmental paths according to location and signals. Cell division supplies material for growth, while later expansion and differentiation determine how that material contributes to the organ.'],
    ['Specialize the wall and contents','Parenchyma often retains living contents and relatively thin primary walls, while supportive cells may strengthen their walls differently. Lignified conducting elements can function after losing their protoplast. The relationship between wall construction and survival therefore varies with the particular cell type and role.'],
    ['Integrate different cell types','A vascular bundle or tissue contains cooperating cells rather than one uniform design. Living phloem transport cells depend on metabolic support, whereas xylem conducting elements use a different architecture. Classifying the full tissue requires its arrangement and functions as well as the appearance of an individual cell.']
  ],misconception:['Every thick-walled plant cell is dead, and all xylem cells are identical.','Wall thickness does not by itself determine whether a cell is living. Xylem is a complex tissue with several cell types, including living parenchyma, so a tissue-level label cannot replace examination of its component cells.'],investigation:{title:'Interpret cell features in context',context:'Synthetic observations of three idealized plant-cell types; these are teaching categories, not diagnostic microscope measurements.',columns:['Cell type','Wall feature','Living contents at maturity'],rows:[['A','Thin primary wall','Present'],['B','Uneven thickened primary wall','Present'],['C','Thick lignified conducting wall','Absent']],question:'Which observation rejects the claim that every thick-walled cell is dead, and why is tissue location still needed?',hint:'Find a cell with both a strengthened wall and living contents.',answer:'B has a thickened primary wall and living contents, contradicting the universal claim. Tissue position and neighboring cells are still needed to interpret the full organ-level function.',reasoning:['Type B combines support-related wall thickening with retained living contents.','Type C shows a different combination in a specialized conducting example.','A complex tissue can contain several cell types with complementary functions.'],limitation:'These invented categories simplify variation and cannot identify every tissue from wall appearance alone.'},transfer:['Why might removing a narrow ring of phloem affect roots even if xylem water transport initially continues?','Track sugars from sources to below-ground sinks separately from water flow.','Phloem interruption can restrict assimilate delivery to roots despite initially continuing xylem transport. The two systems carry different materials, although their functions ultimately depend on each other.']});

  add({id:'plant-hormones',title:'Plant Hormones',category:'plant-biology',summary:'Explain context-dependent growth and stress responses through interacting plant signals.',aliases:['auxin','cytokinin','gibberellin','abscisic acid','ethylene'],relatedTopics:['tropisms','seed-germination','stomata'],prerequisites:['cell-membrane','protein-synthesis'],sources:['es-plant-signals']},[
    'Plants coordinate growth and responses with chemical signals. Auxins, cytokinins, gibberellins, abscisic acid and ethylene have overlapping roles. A hormone’s effect depends on the receiving tissue and conditions, not simply its name.',
    'A chemical signal is not a universal grow button. The same signal can produce different outcomes in a root, a shoot or a seed because their receptors, developmental programs and other signals differ. Context turns detection into a particular response.',
    'Follow signal production, transport or local movement, receptor action and tissue response. Compare the same signal in two organs. Use separate arrows for a change in hormone concentration and a change in sensitivity to that hormone.',
    'Auxin contributes to growth patterning and directional responses; cytokinins influence division and development. Gibberellins promote selected growth and germination processes. ABA participates in water-stress responses and seed dormancy, while ethylene is a gaseous signal affecting several developmental responses.',
    'Hormone pathways interact with light, temperature, water status and each other. Dose-response relationships can be nonlinear and tissue-dependent. The five traditional groups are not the entire signaling inventory; brassinosteroids, jasmonates, salicylate and strigolactones add important regulatory roles.',
    'A plant experiment should compare matched developmental stages and a range of signal concentrations before declaring that a hormone always stimulates growth. The lesson explains mechanisms rather than recommending agricultural chemical applications or predicting all species from one seedling assay.'
  ],[['Auxin','A class of plant signals involved in development, patterning and many growth responses.'],['Abscisic acid','A plant hormone involved in responses including water stress and seed dormancy.'],['Sensitivity','The response produced by a tissue for a stated signal exposure.']],[['Detect context','Environmental and developmental conditions influence signaling.'],['Signal','Hormone production and distribution change local availability.'],['Interpret','Receptors and interacting pathways shape the tissue response.'],['Respond','Growth, development or physiological behavior changes.']],[
    ['Why can the same hormone affect roots and shoots differently?',['Tissues differ in sensitivity and downstream developmental programs','A hormone changes into a different element in each tissue','Only shoots possess living cells'],0,'Receiving tissues interpret a signal through different sensitivities and regulatory contexts.'],
    ['Which statement about the traditional five plant hormone groups is accurate?',['They are important groups but not the complete list of plant signals','They explain every response independently of the environment','Every group always promotes elongation'],0,'Plant signaling includes additional hormone groups and extensive interactions with environmental inputs.'],
    ['Which response is associated with ABA in a water-stress context?',['Promotion of stomatal closure signaling','Mandatory opening of every stoma','Conversion of every leaf into a seed'],0,'ABA contributes to guard-cell regulation that reduces water loss during water stress.']
  ],{curiosity:'Why does the same signal have different effects in different organs?',objectives:['Identify contrasting roles of major plant hormone groups.','Distinguish hormone concentration from tissue sensitivity.','Use a dose-response comparison without claiming a universal effect.'],mechanism:[
    ['Establish a signal distribution','Plant hormone availability reflects local synthesis, transport, conversion and breakdown. Unlike a diagram with one central control gland, signaling can originate in multiple tissues. A measured concentration therefore represents a changing distribution rather than a permanent command issued from one location.'],
    ['Interpret in a receiving tissue','Receptors and downstream networks determine the response to a signal. Root and shoot cells can respond differently at the same concentration because their sensitivities and developmental contexts differ. Interactions with other hormones further prevent a single substance from specifying one universal outcome.'],
    ['Integrate growth and environment','Light, water status and developmental stage interact with hormonal regulation. A response that supports elongation under one condition may change under another. Testing a mechanism therefore requires matched tissues and environmental controls, not simply observing that a treated plant differs from an untreated one.']
  ],misconception:['Auxin always makes every plant organ elongate faster.','Auxin effects depend on concentration, tissue sensitivity and developmental state. A dose that promotes one organ’s elongation can inhibit another response, so a single positive growth observation cannot be generalized to every tissue or condition.'],investigation:{title:'One signal, two dose responses',context:'Synthetic elongation results from matched root and shoot models over the same interval; concentrations are arbitrary signal units.',columns:['Signal dose (units)','Root extension (mm)','Shoot extension (mm)'],rows:[['0','4','4'],['1','5','6'],['10','2','8']],question:'Which dose demonstrates a tissue-dependent effect, and why should the result not be described as universal growth promotion?',hint:'Compare each organ with its own zero-dose reference at the highest dose.',answer:'At ten units, root extension is two millimeters below its control while shoot extension is four above. The opposite responses show tissue dependence in this model, not universal stimulation.',reasoning:['Both zero-dose preparations extend four millimeters in the stated interval.','The high-dose root extends less while the shoot extends more.','Separate sensitivities or downstream regulation could explain the contrasting dose responses.'],limitation:'These invented responses illustrate context dependence, not a calibrated hormone recommendation for any plant species.'},transfer:['Would a hormone-insensitive mutant necessarily have a lower hormone concentration?','Distinguish the quantity of a signal from the ability to respond to it.','No. Impaired receptors or downstream signaling can reduce a response even when hormone concentration is unchanged or increased through feedback. Both signal amount and tissue responsiveness need investigation.']});

  add({id:'tropisms',title:'Tropisms',category:'plant-biology',summary:'Connect directional stimuli, asymmetric growth and organ-specific responses.',aliases:['phototropism','gravitropism','geotropism','thigmotropism'],relatedTopics:['plant-hormones','plant-tissues','stomata'],prerequisites:['plant-hormones'],sources:['es-plant-signals']},[
    'A tropism is a growth response oriented relative to a stimulus. Shoots can bend toward light and roots can grow with gravity. The bend forms because the two sides grow differently, not because the plant decides where to travel.',
    'A longer side forces a growing organ to curve toward its shorter side. A small difference in cell expansion, repeated along a region, can therefore redirect a whole shoot without muscles, a nervous system or purposeful movement.',
    'Mark the direction of light or gravity, compare signal distribution across the organ and then compare cell expansion. Follow curvature over time. This schematic exaggerates side differences so the causal sequence is visible.',
    'In shoot phototropism, blue-light sensing contributes to asymmetric auxin distribution and differential elongation. Gravity sensing involves sedimenting starch-rich organelles in specialized cells. Auxin redistribution produces organ-dependent growth effects, helping roots and shoots bend in different directions.',
    'A directional growth response differs from a nastic response whose movement direction is not determined in the same way by stimulus direction. Gravity, light and touch pathways interact with growth zones and tissue sensitivity. Auxin should not be described as equally promoting elongation in every organ.',
    'A rotated-seedling experiment should record orientation and growth over time, with an unrotated control. A single curved plant does not establish which stimulus caused the curve, particularly when light and gravity directions were changed together.'
  ],[['Tropism','A growth response oriented in relation to a directional stimulus.'],['Phototropism','Directional growth in response to light.'],['Gravitropism','Directional growth in response to gravity.']],[['Sense direction','Specialized sensing processes detect a directional cue.'],['Redistribute','Signals become unequal across a responsive organ.'],['Grow asymmetrically','Opposite sides expand at different rates.'],['Bend','Differential extension changes the organ’s orientation.']],[
    ['What directly produces a bend in a growing shoot?',['Unequal extension on its two sides','A conscious choice by the leaf','Equal extension on both sides at all times'],0,'Differential extension changes geometry so the organ curves toward its shorter side.'],
    ['Why can roots and shoots bend differently after a gravity stimulus?',['Their growth responses to redistributed signals differ','Gravity reverses direction inside roots','Roots contain no cells'],0,'Organ-specific sensitivity helps translate signal asymmetry into different directional growth responses.'],
    ['Which experimental change most directly tests light direction?',['Change light direction while keeping gravity orientation fixed','Change light, temperature and orientation together','Compare unrelated plants at one time only'],0,'Holding other factors constant makes the altered light direction a clearer candidate cause.']
  ],{curiosity:'How does unequal growth redirect an organ?',objectives:['Define a directional growth response without implying intention.','Relate asymmetric signaling to curvature.','Design a comparison that separates light from gravity.'],mechanism:[
    ['Detect a directional cue','Photoreceptors or gravity-sensing cells provide information about stimulus direction. In a gravity response, sedimenting starch-rich structures contribute to sensing in particular cell types. The sensing stage must be distinguished from the later growth response because blocking either can prevent the visible bend.'],
    ['Create different growth conditions','Directional information alters signaling across the responsive organ. In a shoot, auxin redistribution can support greater elongation on one side under suitable conditions. Root responses differ in sensitivity, so the same simplified rule about auxin promoting growth cannot be transferred unchanged to both organs.'],
    ['Turn differential extension into curvature','As one side extends more, the organ bends toward the shorter side. The process depends on a living growth region and takes time. A final photograph records accumulated curvature but cannot by itself show the sequence of sensing, signal redistribution and cell expansion that produced it.']
  ],misconception:['A shoot bends toward light because cells on the lit side always grow faster.','In the familiar shoot phototropism example, greater elongation on the shaded side contributes to bending toward the light. The important mechanical principle is unequal extension; identifying the faster side is necessary before explaining the direction of curvature.'],investigation:{title:'Compare growth on opposite sides',context:'Synthetic extension measurements in a short shoot segment during one interval; unequal extension is isolated from other mechanical effects.',columns:['Lighting','Lit-side extension (mm)','Shaded-side extension (mm)'],rows:[['Uniform illumination','2','2'],['Light from one side','2','4'],['Signal redistribution blocked','2','2']],question:'Which condition predicts curvature toward the light, and why does the blocked condition not prove that light was undetected?',hint:'Find the longer side, then separate stimulus detection from downstream growth signaling.',answer:'One-sided light produces greater shaded-side extension, predicting bending toward the lit side. Blocking redistribution can interrupt the response after detection, so lack of bending does not prove lack of sensing.',reasoning:['Equal extension in uniform illumination predicts no side-to-side bending from this mechanism.','The shaded side extends two millimeters more under directional light.','A downstream signaling block can prevent curvature even with intact photoreceptors.'],limitation:'These invented side lengths are a qualitative geometry model and do not determine a precise curvature angle.'},transfer:['Why compare rotated and unrotated seedlings under uniform, non-directional illumination when investigating a gravity response?','Ask whether rotation would otherwise change light direction relative to the organ.','Uniform illumination removes a directional light cue, while the unrotated control helps reveal changes unrelated to rotation. Keeping a directional lamp fixed would still change light relative to the rotated organ. Matched conditions reduce that confound but do not alone prove the sensing mechanism.']});

  add({id:'plant-reproduction',title:'Plant Reproduction',category:'plant-biology',summary:'Trace alternating generations and distinguish spores, gametes, seeds and vegetative offspring.',aliases:['alternation of generations','sporophyte','gametophyte','double fertilization'],relatedTopics:['meiosis','pollination','seed-germination'],prerequisites:['meiosis','mitosis'],sources:['es-plant-reproduction','es-asexual']},[
    'Plants can reproduce sexually and, in many cases, through vegetative growth. Their sexual life cycle alternates between a generation with two chromosome sets and one with a single set. In flowering plants, the gamete-producing generation is small and dependent.',
    'Meiosis and fertilization are opposite transitions in chromosome-set number, but plant life cycles place growth between them. A spore can divide into a gametophyte before gametes are produced. That intermediate generation is easy to miss in a flowering plant.',
    'Trace a diploid sporophyte through meiosis to haploid spores, then through mitosis to gametophytes and gametes. Return through fertilization to a diploid zygote. Keep seeds and spores distinct: a seed contains a young sporophyte and supporting structures.',
    'In angiosperms, pollen develops from microspores and the embryo sac from a megaspore lineage. Gametes form within these gametophytes. Double fertilization usually yields a diploid embryo and triploid endosperm in a diploid plant. Vegetative reproduction can produce offspring without gamete fusion.',
    'The dominant generation and dependence between generations vary among plant groups. Gametophyte size does not determine its ploidy or importance. Vegetative offspring often preserve a parental genotype, but mutation and environmental effects mean they need not be identical in every observable trait.',
    'Propagation by runners or cuttings and reproduction through seeds make different genetic and ecological tradeoffs. Comparing them requires attention to variation, dispersal and establishment, not a claim that one mode is always biologically superior.'
  ],[['Sporophyte','The diploid generation that produces spores through meiosis.'],['Gametophyte','The haploid generation that produces gametes through mitosis.'],['Spore','A reproductive cell that can develop without fusion with another gamete.']],[['Produce spores','Meiosis reduces chromosome sets in the sporophyte lineage.'],['Grow a gametophyte','Haploid spores undergo mitotic development.'],['Form gametes','Gametes arise within the haploid generation.'],['Fertilize and develop','Fusion forms a zygote that develops into the next sporophyte.']],[
    ['What does plant meiosis directly produce in the alternation-of-generations cycle?',['Haploid spores','A diploid zygote','A mature seed in one division'],0,'Spores produced by meiosis develop into gametophytes, which then produce gametes.'],
    ['How are gametes produced within a haploid gametophyte?',['By mitosis','By a second reduction from diploid to haploid','By combining two embryos'],0,'The gametophyte is already haploid, so gamete production does not require another reduction division.'],
    ['What is a reasonable expectation for vegetative reproduction?',['Offspring often preserve the parental genotype, apart from changes such as mutation','Every offspring combines two independent gamete genomes','All offspring must have identical traits in every environment'],0,'Vegetative propagation often preserves genotype, while mutation and environment can still generate differences.']
  ],{curiosity:'Where does the hidden haploid generation fit?',objectives:['Trace ploidy through a plant sexual life cycle.','Distinguish spores, gametes and seeds.','Compare sexual and vegetative reproduction without universal claims.'],mechanism:[
    ['Make a haploid starting point','The diploid sporophyte produces spores through meiosis. These spores are not already fertilized embryos and do not have to fuse before developing. A correct life-cycle diagram therefore places a haploid developmental stage between reduction division and the eventual production of gametes.'],
    ['Develop the gamete-producing generation','A spore undergoes mitotic development into a gametophyte. In flowering plants this generation is reduced to small structures associated with pollen and the embryo sac. Its small size does not change its haploid status or remove its role in producing the gametes.'],
    ['Restore the sporophyte generation','Fertilization restores a diploid zygote that develops into a new sporophyte. Angiosperm double fertilization also initiates a nutritive endosperm lineage under the usual diploid-plant model. Vegetative propagation bypasses gamete fusion, providing a different route that usually preserves much of the parental genetic combination.']
  ],misconception:['Plant meiosis makes seeds directly, just as animal meiosis makes gametes.','In the plant alternation-of-generations cycle, meiosis makes spores. Spores develop into gametophytes that produce gametes, and seeds form later around a developing embryo. Collapsing these stages removes both a generation and the fertilization step.'],investigation:{title:'Track chromosome sets through generations',context:'Theoretical chromosome accounting for a fictional diploid flowering plant with 2n = 12; assume ordinary meiosis and fertilization.',columns:['Stage','Chromosome number','Chromosome sets'],rows:[['Sporophyte cell before replication','12','2n'],['Spore after meiosis','6','n'],['Gametophyte cell','6','n'],['Zygote before replication','12','2n']],question:'How many chromosomes should a gamete contain, and why does gametophyte growth not restore the diploid number?',hint:'Follow mitosis within the already haploid generation before considering fusion.',answer:'A gamete contains six chromosomes. Gametophyte growth uses mitosis and preserves the haploid set; combining two gamete contributions at fertilization restores twelve chromosomes.',reasoning:['Meiosis reduces the fictional plant from twelve chromosomes to six per spore.','Mitotic gametophyte development preserves that chromosome-set count rather than halving it again.','Fertilization combines two six-chromosome contributions to restore the diploid complement.'],limitation:'This idealized cycle excludes polyploidy, unusual life cycles, apomixis and chromosome-segregation errors.'},transfer:['Why is a seed not equivalent to a single haploid spore?','Compare developmental contents and the event that preceded embryo formation.','A seed contains a developing sporophyte embryo and associated protective or nutritive structures, usually following fertilization. A haploid spore precedes gametophyte development and is not that multicellular seed package.']});

  add({id:'pollination',title:'Pollination',category:'plant-biology',summary:'Separate pollen transfer, compatibility, pollen-tube growth and double fertilization.',aliases:['pollen','stigma','pollen tube','self pollination','cross pollination'],relatedTopics:['plant-reproduction','seed-germination','natural-selection'],prerequisites:['plant-reproduction'],sources:['es-pollination','es-plant-reproduction']},[
    'Pollination moves pollen to a receptive surface. In flowering plants that surface is the stigma. Arrival is not fertilization: compatible pollen must germinate and deliver sperm through a pollen tube before fusion can occur inside an ovule.',
    'Transporting a message to a doorway does not guarantee successful delivery inside. Pollen arrival, compatibility and tube growth are separate filters. A flower can receive many pollen grains yet form few seeds when a later step is limited.',
    'Follow pollen from anther to stigma, then a growing tube through the style toward an ovule. Show two sperm deliveries separately from pollen transfer. The schematic distinguishes a pollen grain, which is a gametophyte, from the sperm it carries.',
    'Wind, animals or other agents can transfer pollen. Compatible pollen germinates and tube growth directs sperm toward the embryo sac. In typical angiosperm double fertilization, one sperm fuses with the egg and another with the central cell, initiating embryo and endosperm lineages.',
    'Self-incompatibility can limit fertilization by genetically incompatible pollen, while pollinator behavior shapes transfer among plants. In a usual diploid angiosperm model, the embryo is 2n and the primary endosperm nucleus is 3n. Seed set can also be limited after fertilization by resources or embryo survival.',
    'A pollinator-exclusion experiment can test whether visitors contribute to seed production, but bagging may alter humidity or temperature. Hand-pollination and handling controls help distinguish pollen delivery from these side effects. One species does not represent every pollination strategy.'
  ],[['Stigma','The pollen-receptive region of a flower’s carpel.'],['Pollen tube','A growing extension of the pollen gametophyte involved in sperm delivery.'],['Double fertilization','Two fusion events initiating embryo and endosperm lineages in angiosperms.']],[['Transfer','Pollen reaches a stigma by a transport mechanism.'],['Recognize','Compatibility and receptivity influence successful germination.'],['Deliver','The pollen tube grows toward an ovule.'],['Fuse','Separate fusion events initiate embryo and nutritive tissue development.']],[
    ['Why is pollen arrival not the same event as fertilization?',['Compatibility, tube growth and gamete fusion must still occur','Each pollen grain is already a diploid embryo','A stigma contains no living cells'],0,'Pollination supplies pollen, while fertilization requires subsequent sperm delivery and fusion.'],
    ['In the usual diploid flowering-plant model, what is the primary endosperm ploidy?',['Triploid','Always haploid','Always identical to the pollen grain'],0,'A sperm contribution joins the central-cell maternal contributions, commonly producing a triploid nucleus.'],
    ['Why might abundant pollen still fail to produce many seeds?',['Compatibility or post-pollination processes may limit success','Every landed pollen grain must become one seed','Pollen transfer instantly removes all resource constraints'],0,'Seed formation depends on several later steps as well as pollen arrival and quantity.']
  ],{curiosity:'Why can a flower receive pollen but form few seeds?',objectives:['Separate pollination from sperm delivery and fusion.','Explain typical double-fertilization products.','Interpret pollinator-exclusion data with appropriate controls.'],mechanism:[
    ['Move pollen between structures','Pollen must be transported from its source to a receptive stigma in a flowering plant. Wind and animals provide different routes, and self-pollination can occur in suitable species. The number arriving describes transfer, not the number that will complete fertilization or form seeds.'],
    ['Pass compatibility and growth stages','Compatible pollen can hydrate, germinate and produce a tube that grows toward an ovule. Receptivity and compatibility affect this sequence, so even a large pollen deposit may not ensure sperm delivery. Tube growth is an active cellular process rather than pollen falling directly onto an egg.'],
    ['Complete two fusions and develop','In typical angiosperms, one sperm contribution joins the egg and another joins the central cell. These events initiate embryo and endosperm lineages, usually diploid and triploid in the stated diploid model. Subsequent survival and resource allocation still influence whether a mature seed ultimately forms.']
  ],misconception:['Each pollen grain landing on a stigma automatically becomes a seed.','Pollen arrival must be followed by compatible germination, tube growth, sperm delivery and fusion. Even after fertilization, resource limits or developmental failure may reduce mature seed production, so arrival counts cannot be equated with seed counts.'],investigation:{title:'Test a contribution from visitors',context:'Synthetic counts of mature seeds per matched group of ten flowers in one fictional insect-visited species.',columns:['Treatment','Seeds counted'],rows:[['Open access','80'],['Visitor exclusion','20'],['Exclusion plus compatible hand pollen','70']],question:'How much does hand pollen increase seed count under exclusion, and what does that support without proving?',hint:'Compare the two excluded groups while retaining possible handling and microclimate effects.',answer:'Hand pollen increases the count by fifty seeds, from twenty to seventy. This supports a pollen-delivery contribution but does not prove that visitors are the only limitation on seed formation.',reasoning:['The two excluded groups share the stated visitor barrier.','Adding compatible pollen increases the count substantially within that comparison.','Handling controls and environmental measurements are needed to exclude additional treatment effects.'],limitation:'These invented counts lack replicate variation and cannot establish universal pollinator dependence or statistical significance.'},transfer:['Would removing animal visitors necessarily prevent reproduction in every flowering plant?','Consider wind pollination and compatible self-pollination as alternative routes.','No. Some plants use wind or other agents, and some can self-pollinate compatibly. The consequence depends on the species, its compatibility system and alternative pollen-transfer routes.']});

  add({id:'seed-germination',title:'Seed Germination',category:'plant-biology',summary:'Connect hydration, dormancy, reserve mobilization and seedling establishment.',aliases:['imbibition','radicle','dormancy','seedling'],relatedTopics:['plant-hormones','cellular-respiration','plant-reproduction'],prerequisites:['cellular-respiration','plant-reproduction'],sources:['es-pollination','es-plant-signals']},[
    'A seed contains an embryo and resources that can support its first growth. Germination begins after suitable conditions allow development to resume. Water is important, but a living seed may also need particular temperature, oxygen or dormancy-breaking cues.',
    'A hydrated seed is not automatically ready to grow. Its internal state and environmental cues must agree. Dormancy can delay development even when water is present, reducing the risk of starting a seedling during conditions it cannot survive.',
    'Trace water uptake, renewed metabolism, reserve mobilization and radicle emergence. Then show the seedling reaching light and becoming more photosynthetically self-supporting. Separate germination from later establishment so an emerged root is not treated as a mature plant.',
    'Imbibition restores water availability and supports metabolic activity. Respiration supplies ATP as stored carbohydrates, lipids or proteins are mobilized. Dormancy and germination involve interacting signals including ABA and gibberellins. Radicle protrusion is commonly used as an operational germination endpoint.',
    'Requirements differ among species and seed states: light, temperature history, seed-coat properties and oxygen availability can all matter. In cereal examples, gibberellin signaling can promote reserve-mobilizing enzymes. Non-germination alone cannot distinguish dormancy from nonviability or unsuitable conditions.',
    'A seed trial should record species, seed lot, conditions, replicates and the endpoint used. Reporting the percentage with emerged radicles is more precise than saying a treatment makes seeds healthy. The example data here are invented and are not a cultivation prescription.'
  ],[['Imbibition','Water uptake by a dry seed or other water-absorbing material.'],['Dormancy','An internal state preventing germination under otherwise permissive conditions.'],['Radicle','The embryonic root, often the first structure to emerge during germination.']],[['Hydrate','The seed takes up water under suitable conditions.'],['Resume metabolism','Respiration and cellular processes become more active.'],['Mobilize reserves','Stored materials support the embryo’s early growth.'],['Emerge and establish','The radicle emerges before later seedling establishment.']],[
    ['Why might a viable seed fail to germinate even when water is available?',['Dormancy or other unmet conditions can prevent germination','Water guarantees germination in every seed','A seed cannot contain an embryo'],0,'Viability, dormancy and environmental requirements are separate contributors to germination success.'],
    ['What commonly provides early growth energy before a seedling photosynthesizes effectively?',['Metabolism of stored seed reserves','Creation of carbon atoms from sunlight alone','Absorption of fully formed leaves from soil'],0,'Stored materials can fuel respiration and biosynthesis during early seedling growth.'],
    ['What does recording radicle emergence directly measure?',['An operational germination endpoint','Guaranteed survival to reproductive maturity','The exact cause of all failed seeds'],0,'Radicle emergence marks a defined stage but does not establish later survival or failure causes.']
  ],{curiosity:'Why is water sometimes not enough to start a seed?',objectives:['Separate viability, dormancy and permissive conditions.','Trace reserve use before effective photosynthesis.','Calculate germination percentages while stating the measured endpoint.'],mechanism:[
    ['Hydrate and assess conditions','A dry seed absorbs water through imbibition, enabling biochemical processes to resume. Temperature, oxygen and species-specific cues still affect the response. An internal dormant state may prevent germination despite hydration, so water uptake and developmental commitment should not be treated as identical observations.'],
    ['Mobilize stored resources','The embryo initially depends on materials already stored in the seed. Enzymatic mobilization supplies substrates for respiration and biosynthesis, with hormonal regulation varying among species. In cereal examples, gibberellin-related signaling supports reserve mobilization, but no one pathway describes every seed type or dormancy mechanism.'],
    ['Distinguish emergence from establishment','Radicle protrusion provides a practical endpoint for many germination trials. The emerging seedling must then continue growing and eventually obtain sufficient resources from its environment. A seed can therefore meet the recorded germination endpoint yet fail to establish, making later survival a separate measurement.']
  ],misconception:['A seed that does not germinate immediately must be dead.','Non-germination can result from dormancy or unsuitable conditions as well as nonviability. A single trial without the correct cues cannot distinguish these causes, so the observation should be reported without assuming that every remaining seed is dead.'],investigation:{title:'Define the endpoint before comparing groups',context:'Synthetic radicle-emergence counts after seven days in groups of 40 seeds from the same fictional seed lot.',columns:['Condition','Radicles emerged','Seeds tested'],rows:[['Suitable moisture and air','30','40'],['Low water availability','8','40'],['Waterlogged, oxygen limited','12','40']],question:'What are the three germination percentages, and why do the ungerminated counts not directly reveal how many seeds are dead?',hint:'Divide emerged radicles by tested seeds, then consider other causes of non-emergence.',answer:'The percentages are 75%, 20% and 30%. Non-emergence can reflect unsuitable conditions or dormancy as well as nonviability, so the remaining counts are not counts of dead seeds.',reasoning:['Thirty of forty gives seventy-five percent in the stated reference condition.','Eight and twelve of forty give twenty and thirty percent respectively.','Additional viability and dormancy evidence is needed to interpret the remaining seeds.'],limitation:'The invented groups lack replicate variation and do not establish universal germination requirements or later seedling survival.'},transfer:['Why could a seed germinate in darkness yet its seedling later fail without light?','Separate stored reserves for early growth from sustained resource acquisition.','Stored reserves can support initial germination and early growth. Once those reserves become limiting, many seedlings need effective photosynthesis, so successful radicle emergence does not guarantee continued establishment.']});

